"Saving Foundation Flow-Matching Priors for Inverse Problems (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16520v1)\nciteturn2510view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16520v1\",\"lineno\":146}); Total lines: 507\nL127: ## 3 Method\nL128: \nL129: The goal of this paper is to close the performance gap between foundation FM priors and domain-specific FM & untrained ones identified in cite11†Section 2.3.1 , by addressing the deficiency of the current prior-strengthening ideas summarized in cite12†Section 2.3.2 .\nL130: \nL131: Table 2: Image regression on $1000$ random images from the DIV2K dataset; details in cite31†Section A.2 .\nL132: Metric  | D-Flow  | FMPlug\nL133: --- | --- | ---\nL134: PSNR  | 36.187  | 37.924\nL135: LPIPS  | 0.181  | 0.093\nL136: Between the two approaches to solving IPs with pretrained FM priors (cite9†Section 2.2 ), we follow the plug-in approach as formulated in cite80†Eq. 2.3 , due to its superior performance in practice (see, e.g., cite78†Table 1 and cite22†Section 4 ). For this approach, a potential concern is whether $\\mathcal{G}_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}$ is surjective, i.e., whether every reasonable $\\boldsymbol{x}$ can be represented as $\\mathcal{G}_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}(\\boldsymbol{z})$ for some $\\boldsymbol{z}$.\nL137: While theoretical results of this nature seem lacking and modeling high-dimensional distributions for such theoretical analysis also seem tricky, empirically, the desired surjectivity seems to hold approximately based on our image regression test reported in cite81†Table 2 .\nL138: To strengthen the foundation FM priors, we consider two practical settings: (A) simple-distortion setting, in which $\\boldsymbol{x}$ and $\\boldsymbol{y}$ are close, e.g., for image restoration. This is the setting considered in previous prior-strengthening works (cite66†Ben-Hamu et al., 2024 ; cite64†Kim et al., 2025 ); and (B) few-shot setting, in which a small number of image instances close to $\\boldsymbol{x}$ are provided but $\\boldsymbol{x}$ and $\\boldsymbol{y}$ might not be close.\nL139: This is particularly relevant for IPs arising from scientific imaging, where the image domain is typically very narrow and known with a few samples (cite82†Huang et al., 2022 ; cite83†Shen et al., 2019 ; cite84†Masto et al., 2025 ). For both settings, taking the image instance(s) close to $\\boldsymbol{x}$ as a guide, we develop a time-dependent warm-start strategy and a sharp Gaussian regularization that together lead to convincing performance gains.\nL140: Below, we first assume the simple-distortion setting and describe the warm-start strategy and the Gaussian regularization in cite14†Section 3.1 and cite18†Section 3.2 , respectively; we then discuss how to extend the ideas to deal with the few-shot setting in cite21†Section 3.3 .\nL141: Gaussianity in the source and intermediate distributions of FM models and especially the following celebrated concentration-of-measure (CoM) result for Gaussian vectors are crucial for our method.\nL142: ###### Theorem 3.1 (Concentration of measure in Gaussian random vectors (cite85†Vershynin, 2018 )).\nL143: \nL144: For a $d$-dimensional Gaussian random vector $\\boldsymbol{z}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\boldsymbol{0},\\boldsymbol{I}_{d})$, $\\mathbb{P}[|\\norm{\\mb z}_{2}-\\sqrt{d}|\\geq t]\\leq 2e^{-ct^{2}}$ for a universal constant $c>0$.\nL145: This implies that for a standard Gaussian vector $\\boldsymbol{z}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$, $\\norm{\\mb z}_{2}$ lies sharply in the range $[(1-\\varepsilon)\\sqrt{d},(1+\\varepsilon)\\sqrt{d}]$ with $\\varepsilon\\in o(1)$ with overwhelmingly high probability. In other words, $\\boldsymbol{z}$ lies in an ultra-thin shell around $\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}(\\boldsymbol{0},\\sqrt{d})$ (a sphere in $\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ centered at $\\boldsymbol{0}$ and with a radius $\\sqrt{d}$).\nL146: ### 3.1 An instance-guided & time-dependent warm-start strategy\nL147: ##### Why is the initialization strategy in D-Flow problematic?\nL148: In the standard FM setting, the source distribution $\\boldsymbol{z}_{0}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\boldsymbol{0},\\boldsymbol{I})$, whereas the initialized $\\boldsymbol{z}_{0}$ in cite86†Eq. 2.4 has a distribution $\\mathcal{N}(\\sqrt{\\alpha}\\boldsymbol{y}_{0},(1-\\alpha)\\boldsymbol{I})$. One might not worry about this distribution mismatch, as both are supported on the entire ambient space in theory.\nL149: But finite-sample training with polynomially many samples in practice causes a significant gap: due to CoM of Gaussian vectors (cite87†Theorem 3.1 ), virtually all training samples drawn from $\\mathcal{N}(\\boldsymbol{0},\\boldsymbol{I})$ come from an ultra-thin shell $\\mathcal{S}$ around $\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}(\\boldsymbol{0},\\sqrt{d})$,^{1}^{1} 1 To be precise, for $m$ iid drawn Gaussian vectors $\\boldsymbol{z}_{1},\\dots,\\boldsymbol{z}_{m}$, $\\mathbb{P}[\\exists i\\in\\left\\{1,\\dots,m\\right\\}\\;\\text{with}\\;|\\norm{\\mb z_i}_{2}-\\sqrt{d}|\\geq t]\\leq 2me^{-ct^{2}}=2e^{-ct^{2}+\\log m}$ by a simple union bound.\nL150: Taking $t=\\varepsilon\\sqrt{d}$, we obtain that $\\mathbb{P}[\\exists i\\in\\left\\{1,\\dots,m\\right\\}\\;\\text{with}\\;|\\norm{\\mb z_i}_{2}-\\sqrt{d}|\\geq\\varepsilon\\sqrt{d}]\\leq 2e^{-c\\varepsilon^{2}d+\\log m}\\leq 2e^{-c\\varepsilon^{2}d/2}$ provided that $m\\leq e^{c\\varepsilon^{2}d/2}$.\nL151: so the generation function $\\mathcal{G}_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}$ is effectively trained on inputs from the domain $\\mathcal{S}$, not the entire ambient space—which implies that the behavior of $\\mathcal{G}_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}$ on $\\mathcal{S}^{c}$, the complement of $\\mathcal{S}$, is largely undetermined.\nL152: Now, samples from $\\mathcal{N}(\\sqrt{\\alpha}\\boldsymbol{y}_{0},(1-\\alpha)\\boldsymbol{I})$ concentrate around another ultra-thin shell around $\\mathbb{S}^{d-1}(\\sqrt{\\alpha}\\boldsymbol{y}_{0},\\sqrt{(1-\\alpha)d})$, which has only a negligibly small intersection with $\\mathcal{S}$ and lies mostly in $\\mathcal{S}^{c}$. So, the initialization in cite86†Eq. 2.4 lies in $\\mathcal{S}^{c}$ with very high probability.\nL153: Given that the behavior of $\\mathcal{G}_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}$ on $\\mathcal{S}^{c}$ can be wild, this initialization strategy is problematic.\nL154: ##### Our time-dependent warm-up strategy\nL155: \nL156: A typical flow of FM models takes the form\nL157: \nL158:  | $\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{z}_{t}=\\alpha_{t}\\boldsymbol{x}+\\beta_{t}\\boldsymbol{z}\\quad\\text{where}\\;\\boldsymbol{z}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\boldsymbol{0},\\boldsymbol{I}),$  |  | (3.1)\nL159: \nL160: where $\\alpha_{t}$ and $\\beta_{t}$ are known functions of $t$ with the property that\nL161:  | $\\displaystyle\\alpha_{t}{\\scriptstyle\\searrow}0,\\beta_{t}{\\scriptstyle\\nearrow}1\\;\\text{as}\\;t\\to 0,\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\alpha_{t}{\\scriptstyle\\nearrow}1,\\beta_{t}{\\scriptstyle\\searrow}0\\;\\text{as}\\;t\\to 1,$  |  | (3.2)\nL162: where $\\scriptstyle\\nearrow$ and $\\scriptstyle\\searrow$ indicate monotonically increasing and decreasing, respectively. Now, when $\\boldsymbol{x}$ and $\\boldsymbol{y}$ are close, $\\boldsymbol{x}=\\boldsymbol{y}+\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$ for some small (i.e., $\\norm{\\mb\\eps}$ is small compared to $\\norm{\\mb x}$ and $\\norm{\\mb z}$) but unknown $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$. So, we can write the flow as\nL163:  | $\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{z}_{t}=\\alpha_{t}(\\boldsymbol{y}+\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon})+\\beta_{t}\\boldsymbol{z}=\\alpha_{t}\\boldsymbol{y}+\\beta_{t}\\boldsymbol{z}+\\alpha_{t}\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}\\quad\\text{where}\\;\\boldsymbol{z}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\boldsymbol{0},\\boldsymbol{I}).$  |  | (3.3)\nL164: \nL165: To eliminate the unknown $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$, we can approximate the exact flow in cite88†Eq. 3.3 with the following approximate flow\nL166:  | $\\displaystyle\\boldsymbol{z}_{t}\\approx\\alpha_{t}\\boldsymbol{y}+\\beta_{t}\\boldsymbol{z}\\quad\\text{where}\\;\\boldsymbol{z}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\boldsymbol{0},\\boldsymbol{I})$  |  | (3.4)\nL167: with an approximation error $\\alpha_{t}\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$. To control the error, (1) if $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$ is relatively large, a small $\\alpha_{t}$ is desirable; (2) if $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$ is already relatively small, a relatively large $\\alpha_{t}$ is acceptable. So, although we do not know $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$ itself and hence its magnitude, with appropriate $\\alpha_{t}$ we can always make $\\alpha_{t}\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$ sufficiently small.\nL168: So, we leave $\\alpha_{t}$ as a learnable parameter. Since $\\alpha_{t}$ is a known function of $t$, we simply need to leave $t\\in[0,1]$ learnable, leading to our warm-start formulation\nL169:  | $\\displaystyle\\min\\nolimits_{\\boldsymbol{z},t\\in[0,1]}\\;\\ell\\pqty{\\mb y, \\mc A \\circ\\mc G_{\\mb\\theta} \\paren{\\alpha_t \\mb y + \\beta_t \\mb z, t}}.$  |  | (3.5)\nL170: Here we overload the notation of $\\mathcal{G}_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}$ as $\\mathcal{G}_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}:\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\times[0,1]\\to\\mathbb{R}^{d}$—the second input is the current $t$ on the path (the notation in cite80†Eq. 2.3 assumes $t=0$).\nL171: In other words, due to the closeness of $\\boldsymbol{x}$ and $\\boldsymbol{y}$, we do not need to start from scratch, i.e., from a random sample drawn from the source distribution; instead, we plug $\\boldsymbol{y}$ into an appropriate, learnable time point of the flow to create a shortcut.\nL172: Our formulation in cite89†Eq. 3.5 can easily be generalized to latent FM models that are commonly used in practice—we just need to replace $\\mathcal{A}\\circ\\mathcal{G}_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}$ with $\\mathcal{A}\\circ\\mathcal{D}\\circ\\mathcal{G}_{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}$ for the decoder $\\mathcal{D}$ in use. Moreover, it is not only grounded in theory and effective in practice (see cite22†Section 4 ), but also speeds up learning as $t>0$ implies shorter flows, although improving speed is not our current focus.\nL173: ##### Additional mean-variance calibration\nL174: Due to approximation errors in matching the ideal flow during FM training, as well as when approximating cite88†Eq. 3.3 using cite90†Eq. 3.4 , the distribution of $\\boldsymbol{z}_{t}$ could be slightly off the ideal distribution.\nL175: To rectify this, we perform a scalar mean-variance calibration in our implementation: we first draw $4000$ unconditional samples from the foundation FM model and estimate the scalar mean and variance of all coordinates for each time step on the FM model’s time grid; we then fit the data using a lightweight neural network, which predicts mean and variance as a continuous function of $t\\in[0,1]$, to be compatible with our continuous optimization in cite89†Eq. 3.5 . Our mean-variance calibration follows\nL176:  | $$\\widehat{\\boldsymbol{z}}_{t}=\\sqrt{\\sigma^{2}(Z_{t})/\\sigma^{2}(\\boldsymbol{z}_{t})}\\cdot(\\boldsymbol{z}_{t}-\\mu(\\boldsymbol{z}_{t}))+\\mu(Z_{t}),$$  |  | (3.6)\nL177: \nL178: where $\\mu(Z_{t})$ and $\\sigma^{2}(Z_{t})$ are the scalar mean and variance predicted by the neural network, and $\\mu(\\boldsymbol{z}_{t})$ and $\\sigma^{2}(\\boldsymbol{z}_{t})$ are the scalar mean and variance for $\\boldsymbol{z}_{t}$ across all coordinates.\nL179: ### 3.2 A sharp Gaussianity regularization\nL180: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBeyond Tokens in Language Models: Interpreting Activations through Text Genre Chunks (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16540v1)\nciteturn2510view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16540v1\",\"lineno\":65}); Total lines: 269\nL51: Recent work on investigating language model activations and Representation Engineering (cite32†Zou et al., 2023 ; cite33†Burns et al., 2022 ; cite34†Turner et al., 2023 ; cite35†Gurnee & Tegmark, 2023 ; cite36†Meng et al., 2022 ), suggests that it is possible to extract human-understandable concepts with their activations. These results support the hypothesis that a top-down approach to language model interpretability like the method described in this paper is tractable.\nL52: In addition, research on modularity and activation sparsity in language models (cite37†Liu et al., 2023 ; cite38†Pochinkov & Schoots, 2023 ; cite39†Zhang et al., 2022 ; cite40†Zhang et al., 2023b ; cite41†Pfeiffer et al., 2023 ), as well as the achievement of high pruning ratios for task-specific models, (cite42†Xu et al., 2022 ) suggest there may be separable ways of looking at different components of text.\nL53: Research on image models, including interpretability research (cite43†Voss et al., 2021 ; cite44†Olah et al., 2017 ; cite45†Mordvintsev et al., 2018 ), and research into the machine unlearning of specific classes and concepts (cite46†Foster et al., 2023 ; cite47†Bourtoule et al., 2021 ; cite48†Nguyen et al., 2022 ), highlights that it is possible to understand how models activate not only on the specific pixel values, but also on larger scale concepts.\nL54: Our work tries to lay the groundwork for finding these larger scale concepts in text.\nL55: There exists a large variety of research into using language models for text embedding (cite49†Muennighoff et al., 2022 ) and classification (cite50†Minaee et al., 2021 ; cite51†Howard & Ruder, 2018 ) on various datasets and tasks, including news categorisation (cite52†Zhang et al., 2015 ), sentiment analysis (cite53†Maas et al., 2011 ), question answering (cite54†Rajpurkar et al., 2016 ), semantic relatedness (cite55†Marelli et al., 2014 ), and retrieval augmentation (cite56†Thakur et al., 2021 ; cite57†Lin et al., 2022 ).\nL56: However, these are focused on using language models to perform specific tasks, rather than understanding how a language model performs tasks.\nL57: Relatedly, there is research on topic analysis of single texts (cite58†De Paoli, 2023 ) and large corpuses of text (cite59†Gauthier & Wallace, 2022 ), as well as research into qualitative deductive coding, (cite60†Zhang et al., 2023a ; cite61†Gebreegziabher et al., 2023 ; cite62†Rietz & Maedche, 2021 ).\nL58: The research is often narrow in scope, such as finding aggregate themes of outputs from specific online communities (cite63†Tai et al., 2023 ; cite64†Chew et al., 2023 ), classifying types of questions (cite65†Xiao et al., 2023 ), or extracting and reformatting knowledge (cite66†Shi et al., 2023 ).\nL59: Our research, instead of topics, aims to probe into the working of language models to understand on a high-level, how different multi-token scale components are represented in the activations, and thus investigate text categories (cite67†Drew, 2023 ) and block components.\nL60: ## 3 Method\nL61: \nL62: We define chunks as natural units of text that are split into different categories. For the most part, these chunks are analogous to paragraphs, and are simply split by double newlines \\n\\n, though with some few exceptions. For details, see Appendix cite20†A.1 .\nL63: The goal of this work is to investigate the extent to which different classifiers manage to predict the category of a text chunk, given the activations and residual stream that this text generates in a pre-trained model. To this end, we create a dataset and train various classifiers as prediction models. We describe the process and our methods in the following subsections.\nL64: ### 3.1 Datasets\nL65: \nL66: cite68†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Generation of the labelled dataset.\nL67: As shown in Figure cite69†1 , we build our dataset by creating a list of 669 prompts, which are constructed such that they can elicit varied texts from a list of categories and topics. We use them with Mistral-7B-Instruct-v2.0 (cite70†Jiang et al., 2023 ) to generate texts. These generated texts are then passed on to GPT-4 Turbo Preview (cite71†OpenAI, 2024 ), which is asked to segment the text into chunks according to categories and subsequently label each chunk with the selected category.\nL68: We obtain 3914 distinct text chunks. The categories we consider are: instructional, explanatory, speech, narrative, and code. The labelling is reviewed by humans and mislabelled text sections are removed. The prompts and examples of the labelled text are shown in the appendix.\nL69: The category distribution of this synthetic dataset is shown in Table cite72†1 L70: \nL71: Table 1: Distribution of different categories of text chunks.\nL72: Category  | instructional  | explanatory  | speech  | narrative  | code\nL73: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL74: Count  | 1159  | 699  | 548  | 542  | 290\nL75: The primary motivation for using an open-weights model like Mistral-7B-Instruct-v2.0 to generate the texts, is that it allows us to access its activations. This lays the groundwork for future work where we predict the sequence of labels based on the activations produced by the prompt.\nL76: We additionally chose a second, pre-existing classified dataset to compare our dataset agains. The chosen dataset was ‘Corpus of Online Registers of English’ (CORE) (cite24†Laippala et al., 2022 ). From the dataset, we took the overly fine-grained categories and combined them into fewer larger categories, which we used as the single labels for the entire texts. The category distribution of the CORE dataset is shown in Table cite73†2 L77: Table 2: Category distribution of the CORE dataset. Top shows category, bottom shows count.\nL78: News  | Informational  | Opinion  | Sports  | Personal  | Persuasion  | Discussion  | Instructional\nL79: Report  | Report  | Blog\nL80: 6992  | 5917  | 4594  | 1975  | 1849  | 950  | 832  | 214\nL81: ### 3.2 Training procedure\nL82: We work with the Large language model Mistral-7B (cite70†Jiang et al., 2023 ) with 7-billion parameters. The model is accessed via the Hugging Face Transformers library (cite74†Taylor et al., 2022 ) and run using PyTorch (cite75†Paszke et al., 2019 ). We prompt the LLM using text chunks from our two datasets, and we study the residual stream and activations at the output of each attention and MLP layer, as described in (cite76†Radford et al., 2019 ).\nL83: Specifically, a text chunk produces activations $a_{i}^{j}$ in layer $j$ of the LLM for chunk $c_{i}$.\nL84: cite77†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: This figure illustrates the training procedure of prediction models on the task of predicting the category of a text section. The activation $a_{i}^{j}$ of chunk $c_{i}$ in the $j$-th layer is extracted from Mistral-7B.\nL85: The means of activations $a_{i}^{j}$ serve as input for the probe models we train to predict the correct category for each labelled text chunk. We employ a range of commonly used shallow learning classifiers as our probe models from the scikit-learn library cite78†Pedregosa et al. (2011) . We call them using the Lazy Predict library cite79†Pandala & da Silva (2022) , which enables us to test several scikit-learn algorithms on the same dataset.\nL86: We keep the vanilla hyperparameters, apart from max_iter which was fixed at 100000. We scale our data by removing the mean and scaling to unit variance with StandardScaler().\nL87: Probing activations is notoriously subject to all kinds of spurious reasons for high classification accuracy (cite80†Ravichander et al., 2021 ). It demands heavy use of controls in order to validate the study. We drew inspiration from (cite81†Hewitt & Liang, 2019 ) to provide baselines: for each probe, we built a probe trained and tested on Mistral-7B activations and residual stream, but where all the parameters are random. We use the random model probe results as a control task.\nL88: If the probes on the original Mistral-7B show better performance than the control task, this will mean that our probes do not rely on spurious correlation, but on true representations in activations.\nL89: We divide our dataset into 80% for the train set and 20% for the test set.\nL90: \nL91: To evaluate our predictors, we use the Macro F1 score metric (cite82†Grandini et al., 2020 ) to give the same weight to all classes, even in an unbalanced dataset, including both recall and precision.\nL92: ### 3.3 Dimensional Reduction\nL93: \nL94: To study how the model might represent different aspects of text, we use a text embedding model, Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B (cite83†Zhang et al., 2025 ) to encode the split chunks of text. For dimensionality reduction and analysis, we use PHATE (cite84†Moon et al., 2017 ), and show the labeled category. We randomly select 200 samples from each category so as not to overload the display and make it readable.\nL95: \nL96: ## 4 Results\nL97: ### 4.1 Dimensional Reduction\nL98: \nL99: cite85†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3: The PHATE dimensionality reduction for the synthetic dataset. We observe that there is some correspondence between the clusters and the labeled categories. cite86†Image: Refer to caption Figure 4: The PHATE dimensionality reduction for the CORE dataset. We see that there is a lot of overlap between clusters and the labelled categories.\nL100: Looking at Figure cite87†3 , the category does not fully explain the clusters observed in dimensionality reduction, but we see the relatively high correspondence between text categories and various clusters in the embedding space as a promising signal that the encoding of these themes is being captured within the models.\nL101: Looking at Figure cite88†4 , we see a great overlap with clusters that are difficult to distinguish. This could be explained because there are more classes in the corpus dataset, but also because the categories are much more similar in this dataset.\nL102: ### 4.2 Labelling Accuracy\nL103: \nL104: cite89†Image: Refer to caption Figure 5: F1-score performance as a function of the layer fraction for prediction models on the task of predicting the category of a text section. The activation $a_{i}^{j}$ of chunk $c_{i}$ at the $j$-th layer has been extracted from Mistral-7B.\nL105: In Figure cite90†5 we see the macro F1 score performance for various probe models predicting the category. The x-axis indicates the layer from which the activations have been extracted, and the colors indicate the predictor that has been employed. The plain lines correspond to the probing with the test task, whereas the dotted lines correspond to the control task.\nL106: The left graphic shows the performance of probe models based on the activations, the right graphic shows the performance based on the residual stream. The top graphic is based on the synthetic dataset, the bottom graphic is based on the CORE dataset.\nL107: \nL108: We see that the deeper we go in the model, the higher the performances.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEvoVLA/README.md at main · AIGeeksGroup/EvoVLA · GitHub (https://github.com/AIGeeksGroup/EvoVLA/blob/main/README.md)\nciteturn2510view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/AIGeeksGroup/EvoVLA/blob/main/README.md\",\"lineno\":265}); Total lines: 315\nL193: > EvoVLA: Self-Evolving Vision-Language-Action Model\nL194: >\nL195: > cite72†Zeting Liu *, cite73†Zida Yang *, cite74†Zeyu Zhang†steve-zeyu-zhang.github.io *^{†}, and cite75†Hao Tang†ha0tang.github.io ^{#}\nL196: >\nL197: > *Equal contribution. ^{†}Project lead. ^{#}Corresponding author.\nL198: >\nL199: > ECCV 2026\nL200: >\nL201: > ### cite76†Paper†arxiv.org | cite77†Website†aigeeksgroup.github.io | cite78†Data†huggingface.co | cite79†Models†huggingface.co | cite80†HF Paper†huggingface.co L202: \nL203: teaser.mp4\nL204: \nL205: * * *\nL206: ## 🔗 Citation\nL207: \nL208: If you find our work helpful, please cite:\nL209: \nL210:     @article{liu2025evovla,\nL211:       title={EvoVLA: Self-Evolving Vision-Language-Action Model},\nL212:       author={Liu, Zeting and Yang, Zida and Zhang, Zeyu and Tang, Hao},\nL213:       journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2511.16166},\nL214:       year={2025}\nL215:     }\nL216: \nL217: * * *\nL218: \nL219: ## 📰 News\nL220: \nL221:   * [2025-12-05] Code released! We have released the training code, evaluation scripts, and pre-trained models.\nL222:   * [2025-11-27] Paper released on arXiv.\nL223: \nL224: * * *\nL225: ## 🎥 Demo Video\nL226: \nL227: ☝️ Click the image above to watch the High-Res Demo Video (1080P) ☝️\nL228: \nL229: * * *\nL230: ## 📖 Abstract: Ending the Robot's \"Daydream\"\nL231: \nL232: In long-horizon manipulation tasks, robots often suffer from \"Stage Hallucination\": they think they've completed a step because the visual scene looks \"close enough,\" but they haven't actually finished the job (e.g., a block is near the target but not stacked). This \"high confidence, low competence\" failure mode causes catastrophic task failure.\nL233: EvoVLA is a self-evolving framework designed to cure this hallucination. By treating Gemini 2.5 Pro as a strict teacher that generates \"Hard Negatives\" (near-miss scenarios), EvoVLA learns to distinguish between almost done and actually done.\nL234: \nL235: Combined with Pose-Based Object Exploration (POE) and Long-Horizon Memory, EvoVLA achieves SOTA performance on the challenging Discoverse-L benchmark.\nL236: \nL237: * * *\nL238: ## 🚀 Key Features\nL239: ### 1. Stage-Aligned Reward (SAR): The \"Anti-Hallucination\" Mechanism\nL240: \nL241: Traditional rewards are too sparse. We use Gemini to generate a \"Mistake Book\" of Hard Negatives—states that look successful but are actually failures (e.g., \"gripper near object but not touching\").\nL242: \nL243:   * Positive: \"Gripper firmly grasping the block.\"\nL244:   * Hard Negative: \"Gripper near the block, but fingers are empty.\" This forces the VLA to learn precise, stage-aware visual discrimination.\nL245: Figure: The EvoVLA Data Engine generating triplets for contrastive learning.\nL246: ### 2. Pose-Based Object Exploration (POE)\nL247: \nL248: Instead of being curious about pixel changes (which can be noisy shadows or lighting), EvoVLA's curiosity is grounded in geometry. It explores how to change the relative pose between the gripper and the object, leading to efficient, structure-aware discovery.\nL249: ### 3. Long-Horizon Memory\nL250: \nL251: For tasks with 70+ steps, simple memory averaging fails. EvoVLA uses Context Selection to recall only the critical history tokens needed for the current decision, preventing \"catastrophic forgetting.\"\nL252: \nL253: * * *\nL254: \nL255: ## 📊 Performance\nL256: ### Simulation Results (Discoverse-L)\nL257: \nL258: EvoVLA dominates across three tasks of increasing difficulty, especially on the 74-stage Block Bridge task.\nL259: \nL260: Figure: Success rates on Discoverse-L benchmark. EvoVLA (Purple) significantly outperforms OpenVLA and π0.\nL261: \nL262:   * Success Rate: 69.2% (+10.2% vs OpenVLA-OFT)\nL263:   * Hallucination Rate: Reduced from 38.5% to 14.8%\nL264:   * Sample Efficiency: 1.5x faster convergence\nL265: ### Real-World Sim2Real Transfer\nL266: \nL267: Deployed on the AIRBOT-Play robot, EvoVLA shows remarkable robustness.\nL268: \nL269: Figure: Real-world deployment performance.\nL270: \nL271: ### Qualitative Analysis: \"No More Faking It\"\nL272: \nL273: Figure: OpenVLA (Top) opens the gripper too early (hallucination). EvoVLA (Bottom) waits for stable contact.\nL274: \nL275: * * *\nL276: ## 📂 Project Structure\nL277: \nL278:     `EvoVLA/\nL279:     ├── evovla/                    # Core module\nL280:     │   ├── models/                # Policy models (OpenVLA-OFT)\nL281:     │   ├── rewards/               # Reward modules (SAR, POE)\nL282:     │   ├── ppo/                   # PPO trainer\nL283:     │   ├── data/                  # Data utilities (Discoverse wrapper)\nL284:     │   └── utils/                 # Utilities\nL285:     ├── configs/                   # Configuration files\nL286:     ├── scripts/                   # Training and evaluation scripts\nL287:     ├── tools/                     # Data preparation tools\nL288:     └── requirements.txt           # Dependencies\nL289:     `\nL290: ## 🛠️ Quick Start\nL291: \nL292: ### Installation\nL293: \nL294:     # Clone the repository\nL295:     git clone https://github.com/AIGeeksGroup/EvoVLA.git\nL296:     cd EvoVLA\nL297: \nL298:     # Create environment\nL299:     conda create -n evovla python=3.10\nL300:     conda activate evovla\nL301: \nL302:     # Install dependencies\nL303:     pip install -r requirements.txt\nL304: \nL305: ### Training\nL306: \nL307:     # Train on Discoverse-L Bridge Task\nL308:     python train.py --config configs/evovla_bridge.yaml\nL309: \nL310: * * *\nL311: \nL312: ## Star History\nL313: \nL314: You can’t perform that action at this time.\n"
